           "cp": 1
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "t": "p",
        "r": [
         280,
         190,
         720,
         77
        ],
        "x": "Espresso was born in Turin at the end of the nineteenth century, when Angelo Moriondo patented a machine that forced steam and water through a bed of ground coffee. His device was a bulk brewer, but it planted the idea that pressure could trade time for intensity."
       },
       {
        "t": "h2",
        "r": [
         280,
         287,
         720,
         28
        ],
        "x": "From steam to lever"
       },
       {
        "t": "p",
        "r": [
         280,
         335,
         720,
         102
        ],
        "x": "Luigi Bezzera and Desiderio Pavoni refined the concept into single-serving machines, and by 1906 espresso was being served at the Milan World's Fair. The real leap came in 1947 with Achille Gaggia's spring-piston lever, which raised brewing pressure past eight bars and produced crema for the first time."
       },
       {
        "t": "figure",
        "r": [
         320,
         453,
         640,
         36
        ],
        "c": [
         {
          "t": "img",
          "r": [
           320,
           453,
           310,
           18
          ],
          "a": {
           "nm": "A restored 1950s lever espresso machine",
           "alt": "A restored 1950s lever espresso machine",
           "src": "http://127.0.0.1:8901/lever_machine.png"
          }
         },
         {
          "t": "figcaption",
          "r": [
           320,
